                  "outcomes": {
                    "success": "1774596719560"
                  },
                  "inputs": [
                    {
                      "id": "1774596655220_prompt",
                      "name": "prompt",
                      "type": "string",
                      "value": "You are a strict legal policy position matcher for contract clauses.\n\nYour task is to evaluate a contract clause against one policy rule and determine which policy position the clause satisfies.\n\nThe policy rule JSON is the only source of truth.\n\nDo not rely on external legal knowledge.\nDo not rely on wording similarity.\nDo not use close-enough semantic overlap.\nAll evaluation must be based on exact contractual meaning and legal effect.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n**CONTRACT INTENT**\n\n- BuyOrSell: {{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_CONTRACT_METADATA_ATTRIBUTES_VALUES.$output.result.BuyOrSell}}\n- If BuyOrSell = \"B\", contract intent is BUY-INTENT, and tie-breaks must favor BUYER.\n- If BuyOrSell = \"S\", contract intent is SELL-INTENT, and tie-breaks must favor SUPPLIER.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\n**INPUT**\n\nInput Document Clause Title:\n{{$context.$nodes.FOR_EVERY_RULES.EXTRACT_CLAUSE_FOR_CURRENT_RULE.$output.result.clause_title}}\n\nInput Document Clause Text:\n{{$context.$nodes.FOR_EVERY_RULES.EXTRACT_CLAUSE_FOR_CURRENT_RULE.$output.result.clause_text}}\n\nInput Policy Rule JSON:\n{{$context.$nodes.FOR_EVERY_RULES.$currentItem}}\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\n**POLICY STRUCTURE**\n\nPositions include:\n- standard_position\n- fallback_X_position (X = 1..n)\n\nAssociated fields:\n- standard_clause_number\n- fallback_X_clause_number, fallback_X_risk, fallback_X_approval, fallback_X_approval_authority\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- Only position fields (standard_position or fallback_X_position fields) are used for classification/match.\n- Do NOT use fallback_X_clause_number, fallback_X_risk, fallback_X_approval, or fallback_X_approval_authority for exact classification.\n- fallback_X_risk is used only to set severity_code after a fallback position has been selected.\n- Do NOT use fallback_X_risk to choose between multiple matching fallback positions.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\n**STRICT LEGAL MATCHING MODE (NON-NEGOTIABLE)**\n\nFor each position, derive:\n1. mandatory_conditions: every obligation, limitation, qualifier, dependency, and trigger in that position.\n2. prohibited_conditions: any condition that conflicts with or weakens that position.\n3. scope_conditions: actor, timing, trigger, and boundaries, such as only if, unless, provided that, subject to, and solely to the extent.\n\nA position matches ONLY IF:\n- every mandatory_condition is explicitly satisfied somewhere in the input document clause text,\n- no mandatory_condition is missing, diluted, replaced, or contradicted,\n- no prohibited/conflicting/additional risk-shifting condition is introduced anywhere in the input document clause text,\n- scope_conditions are preserved in the same legal effect across the input document clause text.\n\nIf a position has compound qualifiers joined by and / provided / only if / subject to, ALL such qualifiers must be present.\nIf any one qualifier is missing, that position does NOT match.\n\nPartial satisfaction is NOT a match.\n\nDo NOT require exact wording matches.\n- Focus on substantive legal and commercial meaning.\n- Treat synonymous or equivalent legal phrasing as a match when the legal effect is materially the same.\n- Ignore differences in drafting style, tense, formatting, or conversational phrasing unless they materially alter risk allocation or enforceability.\n- Identify the core mandatory elements of each playbook position and determine whether those elements are preserved.\n- Do not classify as STANDARD merely because the standard's minimum requirement is satisfied.\n- A standard_position does not match if the clause adds material legal or commercial conditions beyond the standard_position that change legal effect or more specifically align to a fallback_position.\n\nExample:\nStandard: \"Agreement shall be governed by California law.\"\nFallback 2: \"Agreement shall be governed by California law, with disputes resolved through neutral arbitration.\"\nClause: \"This agreement is governed by California law and disputes will be resolved through neutral arbitration.\"\nBest Match: FALLBACK_2\n\nIf uncertain between a fallback and OUT_OF_POLICY, choose OUT_OF_POLICY.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nSTEP 1 - Input Source Limits\n\nUse ONLY the Input Document Clause Text as the clause to classify.\nUse the Input Policy Rule JSON as the only policy source.\nIgnore formatting-only noise during evaluation: newlines, tabs, repeated spaces, special symbols, and numbering artifacts.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nSTEP 2 - Evaluate All Positions For Exact Match\n\nEvaluate ALL positions independently:\n- standard_position\n- all fallback_X_position fields\n\nFor each position:\n- identify mandatory_conditions, prohibited_conditions, scope_conditions\n- test input document clause text against all of them\n- Conditions may appear anywhere in the input document clause text, including different sentences, paragraphs, subsections, definitions, exceptions, or provisos.\n- Evaluate the clause text as a whole before deciding whether any mandatory_condition is present or missing, any prohibited_condition is present, or any scope_condition is preserved, narrowed, expanded, contradicted, or otherwise changed.\n- mark as MATCH only when all conditions above are satisfied\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nSTEP 3 - Determine Exact Match\n\nIdentify ALL exact matches.\n\nIf NONE match -> set exact_match_result = OUT_OF_POLICY and proceed to STEP 4 before finalizing matched_position_code.\n\nSelection logic:\n- If standard_position matches -> return STANDARD\n- Else if exactly one fallback position matches -> return that fallback\n- Else if multiple fallback positions match:\n  - Do NOT select by fallback_X_risk\n  - Select the matched fallback by commercial favorability to the contract intent party:\n    - BUY-INTENT: choose the fallback more favorable to BUYER.\n    - SELL-INTENT: choose the fallback more favorable to SUPPLIER.\n  - \"More favorable\" means the position gives better rights/protections/economic outcome to the intent party (and/or imposes stronger obligations on the counterparty) for that clause topic.\n  - If still tied after favorability analysis, choose the upper fallback index (FALLBACK_2 over FALLBACK_1, etc.) for deterministic output.\n- Never upgrade a partial/ambiguous/near match to matched fallback\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nSTEP 4 - Closest Fallback Match / Severity Override (ONLY when exact result is OUT_OF_POLICY)\n\nRun this step ONLY if STEP 3 result is OUT_OF_POLICY.\n\nClosest logic rules:\n- Try closest matching ONLY for numeric clause values with one clear numeric dimension.\n- For non-numeric clauses, textual clauses, or ambiguous/unit-unclear extraction -> do NOT attempt closest matching.\n- Closest logic must consider FALLBACK positions only. Ignore STANDARD for closest logic.\n- Determine if the input document clause value is inside the fallback range boundaries for that dimension.\n- If outside fallback range on risky side -> no closest fallback severity override.\n- If inside range, choose the nearest fallback by numeric distance.\n- On numeric-distance tie, follow below guidelines.\n\nCLOSEST FALLBACK SELECTION (when multiple fallbacks are equally close/tie condition)\n- Do NOT break ties by risk.\n- Break ties by commercial favorability to the contract intent party:\n  - BUY-INTENT: choose the fallback more favorable to BUYER.\n  - SELL-INTENT: choose the fallback more favorable to SUPPLIER.\n- \"More favorable\" means the position gives better rights/protections/economic outcome to the intent party (and/or imposes stronger obligations on the counterparty) for that clause topic.\n- If still tied after favorability analysis, choose the upper fallback index (FALLBACK_2 over FALLBACK_1, etc.) for deterministic output.\n\nImportant:\n- When closest fallback severity override is confidently applied, set matched_position_code to the selected closest FALLBACK_X code instead of OUT_OF_POLICY.\n- Set severity_code from the selected closest fallback's fallback_X_risk.\n- The issue field must explain that the clause did not exactly match the selected fallback position, and that the fallback was selected only as the nearest numeric fallback.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nSTEP 5 - Severity (severity_code attribute)\n\nFor exact match path:\n- STANDARD -> 0_NO_RISK\n- FALLBACK_X -> fallback_X_risk\n\nFor OUT_OF_POLICY path:\n- If closest fallback severity override is confidently applied -> severity_code = selected closest fallback's fallback_X_risk\n- Else -> severity_code = 4_CRITICAL\n\nReturn severity_code in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE using mapping:\nNo Risk -> 0_NO_RISK\nLow Risk -> 1_LOW_RISK\nMedium Risk -> 2_MEDIUM_RISK\nHigh Risk -> 3_HIGH_RISK\nCRITICAL -> 4_CRITICAL\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\nSTEP 6 - Issue Explanation (issue attribute)\n\nProvide 2-3 sentences:\n- why the selected position matches, or why OUT_OF_POLICY,\n- which mandatory condition(s) were missing/violated if not standard.\n\nIf closest fallback severity override was applied, explain that the selected fallback is the nearest numeric fallback and not an exact legal match.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\n**OUTPUT**\n\nReturn a valid JSON object only, with the following structure:\n\n{\n  \"policy_deviation\": {\n    \"doc_clause_title\": \"<Exact value of Input Document Clause Title>\",\n    \"rule_clause_title\": \"<clause_title column value from input rule json>\",\n    \"rule_code\": \"<rule_code column value from input rule json>\",\n    \"rule_description\": \"<rule_description column value from input rule json>\",\n    \"matched_position_code\": \"<STANDARD | FALLBACK_X | OUT_OF_POLICY>\",\n    \"issue\": \"<Issue Explanation>\",\n    \"severity_code\": \"<0_NO_RISK | 1_LOW_RISK | 2_MEDIUM_RISK | 3_HIGH_RISK | 4_CRITICAL>\"\n  }\n}\n\n-----------------------------------------------------\n\n**STRICT RULES**\n\n- Output valid JSON only.\n- Use policy rule JSON only.\n- Use input document clause text only as the clause being classified.\n- No wording-similarity-only matching.\n- No partial/approximate/legal-intent-only exact matching.\n- Do NOT use fallback_X_clause_number, fallback_X_approval, or fallback_X_approval_authority for exact classification.\n- Do NOT use fallback_X_risk for exact classification.\n- STANDARD severity_code must always be 0_NO_RISK.\n- Closest fallback logic is disabled for non-numeric or ambiguous clauses.\n- When closest fallback severity override is confidently applied, matched_position_code must be the selected closest FALLBACK_X code.\n- When closest fallback severity override is confidently applied, issue must state that the selected fallback is the nearest numeric fallback, not an exact legal match.\n- doc_clause_title must exactly equal the Input Document Clause Title.\n- Do NOT omit any required output fields."
                    }
                  ],
                  "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\n    \"policy_deviation\"\n  ],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"policy_deviation\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\n        \"doc_clause_title\",\n        \"rule_clause_title\",\n        \"rule_code\",\n        \"rule_description\",\n        \"matched_position_code\",\n        \"issue\",\n        \"severity_code\"\n      ],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"doc_clause_title\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"rule_clause_title\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"rule_code\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"rule_description\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"matched_position_code\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\",\n          \"pattern\": \"^(STANDARD|FALLBACK_[0-9]+|OUT_OF_POLICY)$\"\n        },\n        \"issue\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"severity_code\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\",\n          \"pattern\": \"^(0_NO_RISK|1_LOW_RISK|2_MEDIUM_RISK|3_HIGH_RISK|4_CRITICAL)$\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n"
                },
                {
                  "id": "1777002000102",
                  "code": "LOG_NOT_APPLICABLE_RULE",
                  "type": "CODE",
                  "metadata": {
                    "name": "Log Not Applicable Rule",
                    "returnType": "object",
                    "sourceCode": "const startedAt = Date.now();\nconst contractId = $context.$variables.contractId || null;\nconst rule = $context.$nodes.FOR_EVERY_RULES.$currentItem || {};\nconst app = $context.$nodes.FOR_EVERY_RULES.CHECK_RULE_APPLICABILITY.$output || {};\n\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify({\n  node: 'LOG_NOT_APPLICABLE_RULE',\n  event: 'rule_not_applicable',\n  contractId,\n  rule_code: rule.rule_code || null,\n  clause_title: rule.clause_title || null,\n  matched_position_code: 'NOT_APPLICABLE',\n  status: 'skipped',\n  reason: app.reason || 'Rule aspect not materially present in clause text',\n  durationMs: Date.now() - startedAt,\n  error: null\n}));\n\nconst result = {\n  skipped: true,\n  reason: 'NOT_APPLICABLE',\n  rule_code: rule.rule_code || null,\n  rule_clause_title: rule.clause_title || null\n};\nreturn result;"
                  },
